  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1042 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nHeights will fall on Sunday as the ridge begins to retreat eastward, \nhowever high temperatures remain well above average in the mid 90s \non the Caprock to low triple-digits continuing off the Caprock. \nMonday is trending drier models are now indicating a upper low over \nthe northern Great Plains taking a more northeasterly trajectory and \ndeamplifying as it enters Canada. This flow orientation would keep \nnearly all of the Pacific/monsoonal moisture off to the north and \nwest. Another upper trough will move into the Pacific Northwest on \nTuesday with a low becoming cutoff and stalling over Oregon through \nThursday. As strong ridge over the Southeast will again keep a \nmajority of the moisture to the north and west, however Tuesday \nevening on the Caprock looks to see the greatest chance of storms at \nthis point before the ridge shifts eastward mid-to-late week. NBM \nhas been retained although it is likely a bit heavy-handed as usual \nfor that time frame, however should start dropping with future \nforecasts if current trends continue. Temperatures will start slowly \ndropping each day, potentially into the mid 80s over the far \nsouthwest Panhandle towards the end of the week.",
